### Grammar Parser Function

The grammar parser function uses the generated grammar rules to parse the input sentence into individual tokens (words or phrases).

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.regex.*;

public class GrammarParser {
    public static List<String> parse(String sentence) {
        List<String> tokens = new ArrayList<>();
        Pattern pattern;
        Matcher matcher;

        // Regular expression pattern for matching grammatical terms
        String[] terms = {"noun", "verb", "adjective"};
        pattern = new Pattern(terms, "[a-zA-Z]+");
        matcher = Pattern.compile(pattern);

        while (matcher.find()) {
            tokens.add(matcher.group());
        }

        return tokens;
    }
}

class Pattern implements Matcher {
    private String[] terms;
    private String regex;

    public Pattern(String[] terms, String regex) {
        this.terms = terms;
        this.regex = regex;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean match(Object o) {
        if (o instanceof String) {
            return ((String) o).matches(regex);
        }
        throw new RuntimeException("Invalid input type");
    }
}
```

In this implementation, the `parse` function uses a regular expression pattern to match individual words or phrases in the sentence. The pattern consists of an array of grammatical terms (`noun`, `verb`, and `adjective`) separated by whitespace.

The `Pattern` class implements the `Matcher` interface, which provides methods for matching the pattern against different inputs (in this case, strings).

